  "source_name": "https://www.whattoexpect.com/pregnancy/week-by-week/week-33.aspx",
  "source_type": "website",
  "structure": {
    "block_count": 91,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "33 Weeks Pregnant",
        "text_sha256": "cbd9468552cc787c45ee8a7f9821edd562f2fd4811eb9f4a75e9fd17ac8dce44",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby is as big as a butternut squash.",
        "text_sha256": "dbf6e8e1cd939a759dd921de047471c5b5c9891463dfd83e90d5c5905dbe6072",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Now that you're 33 weeks along, you're approaching the middle of the third trimester .",
        "text_sha256": "f30845e4ad7e520cdf5049427f3d0349ba18a29b4413aab063fdc7115973de3c",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "By now, your baby has reached the length he'll measure at birth, but he's still busy putting on about half a pound a week.",
        "text_sha256": "c7e521c9f26030b3d922f822d080892352020614ab30aaf54462e20e68093863",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You might notice this rapid growth in the form of sharper kicks (oof) and shortness of breath, thanks to crowding around your lungs.",
        "text_sha256": "b15be16272ad8362a59f201bf7f4cf35596b07d1c0416e0cffe2395893186a91",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Baby at Week 33",
        "text_sha256": "703668eac05ad09a17d58620873c930f8b89e8f50d23f1bc841af96e6cfb03da",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "At a Glance",
        "text_sha256": "4007536f1ab858815b5f74377d1a44ff31bd2acc98e17bf65e9e39ed107102a1",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Soft spot",
        "text_sha256": "80c4cb04b917a7f099d960fce020ae6a2ae72b9174a97d4ed98e6b65f34a9442",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The plates of bone in your baby’s skull are still fairly pliable, which makes it easier for him to squeeze down the birth canal.",
        "text_sha256": "6cb6396b45f046c4e6e58017a55873082e7a4e477f67fdcf35bdc1fde5fc5944",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Thirsty baby",
        "text_sha256": "cd1439e9abb9800560fa2464085eae8b8cdbc86f4457d3ff3bf9710f5f306cbc",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby drinks up to a pint of amniotic fluid a day! This helps prepare your baby’s gastrointestinal system for prime time.",
        "text_sha256": "7f01e99c86a5b4f0a07ac451059813a535ecaf1cf88952be51531c5cad6011f5",
        "word_count": 22
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Packing on the pounds",
        "text_sha256": "af122c3855f0925cc2e81123940e9894132ee4bea7c1595bddec9422b5c9bfd4",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your baby is gaining weight at a fast clip now, putting on about half a pound a week. He'll come close to doubling his weight by the time he's born.",
        "text_sha256": "18fac6df87f24c326043390b16e9e489e077808bc3a39570b5eb3eab0f92b328",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "33 weeks pregnant is how many months?",
        "text_sha256": "6f3ccf18a35b7a3f3ca28d21d7097d568ceca17189b875300bac855d444e08f8",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you're 33 weeks pregnant, you're in month 8 of your pregnancy. Only 1 month left to go! Still have questions? Here's some more information on how weeks, months, and trimesters are broken down in pregnancy .",
        "text_sha256": "9b5333c6c4153daf843d8a99687799d43eefaa02bdaa078eda64bf41283ed3c4",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "How big is my baby at 33 weeks?",
        "text_sha256": "d614db19ab7c6a1471f90fc8a28fef05f92b548739b2a3b4daa571c2938e4c3a",
        "word_count": 8
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "This week your baby may be anywhere between 16 and 17 or more inches in length and could grow up to another full inch this week — especially if he's been on the shorter side.",
        "text_sha256": "9428eec3269f68dac7280fefd8e475aac6f64bd0b48a531ac2b2c31fed97b912",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "He weighs more than 4¼ pounds and is still gaining weight at a rate of about half a pound a week. Weight gain can range from a third more to a full doubling before the big debut.",
        "text_sha256": "09682e61a7fef0ae1f1778f34b978451bd59c90468e13fb86c8d8fbe24212760",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Baby's kicks may feel sharper",
        "text_sha256": "a6ac78dce5521453cf3d9a08f9e20d7d34485972fd4ea07bfaf09394210254e3",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "With that much baby inside your uterus, your amniotic fluid level has maxed out at 33 weeks pregnant, making it likely you have more baby than fluid now. That's one reason why some of his pokes and kicks feel pretty sharp these days.",
        "text_sha256": "9e4ab9d4bbd3a3608138af8b5438b5bf7bfa213f999de1f36aba393b5925dea7",
        "word_count": 43
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Baby differentiates day from night",
        "text_sha256": "e8d9f27de78ea6b602de64b1573681474f961fe598bc67b82e0e692b640110fd",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your uterus had eyes, here's what you'd see: your fetus acting more and more like a baby, with his eyes closing during sleep and opening while awake.",
        "text_sha256": "a21a871a91bd09e277eeb8815ad984b43a5512797232623c69ff9ade3ed11deb",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "And because those uterine walls are becoming thinner, more light penetrates the womb, helping your baby differentiate between day and night. Now if only baby can remember that difference on the outside!",
        "text_sha256": "7b668d754cac4af3a9de6f19ec91e7f7018d509b8e7d272f54c2e9fb84b4e764",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Fetal immune system is developing",
        "text_sha256": "95c5c5981f559e2925279dbe355bf354c34d3780628f56cdfa471da1a3ec6727",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Good news! Your baby has reached an important milestone about now: He's got his own immune system. Antibodies are being passed from you to your little one as he continues to develop his fetal immune system, which will come in handy once he's outside the womb and fending off all sorts of germs.",
        "text_sha256": "4abbbb29f7f67bf1aa4a828f6c2945460ffd52ba3c39475f73cbb895ec314938",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Body at Week 33",
        "text_sha256": "e0c1a04e7d2c9352c57f8e01834f2c6610b74ec8168ec94fd9c387c72f455fa9",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Insomnia",
        "text_sha256": "9dcefd14ed11ae5d50e04abac353e8615bb05f00200536c86a107e8bbf62f64e",
        "word_count": 1
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "With the hormonal changes , midnight bathroom runs, leg cramps , heartburn, and your basketball-sized belly, it's no wonder sleep is elusive. Third trimester insomnia strikes about 3 in 4 pregnant women — who may also be coping with anxiety about the upcoming birth and a mind that races all night long thinking about their to-do-before-the-baby-comes list.",
        "text_sha256": "225a8f96165d0b78abfdd6336edb6087028bd4d54d8ef6b09f491b8b9c34d95b",
        "word_count": 57
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "At 33 weeks pregnant, your body needs rest, so remember that worrying about it won't help and neither will staring at the clock watching the minutes tick by.",
        "text_sha256": "2ac96cff6a66c58d0e363b84bb262f0ec98967f11c6f0c032032a8c26dc7bc20",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Instead, do your best to get comfy — before bed and when you get in it. Try a warm bath and perhaps a warm cup of milk before turning in, plus avoid exercising, screen time, eating, or drinking too close to bedtime. You can also try asking your partner for a massage. You deserve it!",
        "text_sha256": "905cb6a23666e4c167f48be1841598c2385b0fbb6ba2404a0968bd97df8e150b",
        "word_count": 55
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If sleep still eludes you, read a book or listen to soothing music until drowsiness sets in. And look on the bright side: Pregnancy insomnia is great training for those sleepless nights to come!",
        "text_sha256": "ff688a4e99cef62ab1a9bff6784b9629ba9746076171bd32fba53e7010d9b5bd",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Omega-3 fatty acids",
        "text_sha256": "39695364c7fcb32456928dae760056629cb6e34a443bd1c5aa55b1093128353b",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Studies suggest that infants born to mothers whose diets contain plenty of omega-3 fatty acids (DHA) found mostly in fish oils have an edge in terms of early development.",
        "text_sha256": "619ae574926057c0f9812869d646351aa5013cf7c02f1c0a7dfbb1d8582ba85a",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "So if you eat your wild salmon, will your kid go to Harvard? Maybe, maybe not. But DHA is critical to brain and vision development — and almost all of a baby's accumulation of DHA occurs during the last trimester. DHA may also help prevent preterm labor and protect against postpartum depression .",
        "text_sha256": "247bdcc16d8d77d530bdeddfdb17b9d821751655b7e53c50f89cc3a2e4f40b30",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But haven't you heard that fish is unsafe for pregnant women? Actually, the Food & Drug Administration (FDA) recommends eating 8 to 12 ounces (or about two to three average meals) a week of a variety of well-cooked fish and shellfish that are low in mercury, such as shrimp, tilapia, flounder, salmon (wild is best), pollack, and catfish.",
        "text_sha256": "625d7b23703944f4ab2a976a6d0ff2ce49c3e263f3b49892760d5e2102031c66",
        "word_count": 58
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Steer clear of swordfish, shark, and bigeye tuna, which are more likely to contain higher levels of mercury.",
        "text_sha256": "c46b6d8cdcda40336de61054923bf1c22a519476549377747d7f61e047451a29",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Hate fish? Other DHA sources include algae-derived supplements, though check your health food store and ask your practitioner before buying a bottle, and DHA-enriched eggs, available in most supermarkets. Or try these tasty omega-3-rich and pregnancy-friendly meal ideas .",
        "text_sha256": "94027027500a68e77d9800a0e3876108ae8e47e71f1a73c75d4c2c4518a6cb3b",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Pregnancy Symptoms Week 33",
        "text_sha256": "794f596944cbbe12d17f2e5910a730226f162dcdc21153426a10fd775ed63fda",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Round ligament pain",
        "text_sha256": "71855151348b5a1791da6fdccf61f5105ad7bb60ca270e6fd5d82f0c1d261ff7",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your belly is aching when you change positions or get up suddenly, you could be suffering from round ligament pain, or growing pains. As long as it’s occasional and you don’t have fever, chills, or bleeding along with it, there’s nothing to worry about. Getting off your feet and getting comfy helps.",
        "text_sha256": "2f2d1c08c2d7f49351e3337726238d8fb1b0fae940fa7b03584c9457df25e74a",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Pregnancy brain",
        "text_sha256": "2b86f7e34fd22a35cc48aab7dd82c2572550cd2007df723dd9b5c75d25dfad7c",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That foggy brain could clue you in to your baby’s sex (though it could be an old wives' tale too). Women pregnant with girls sometimes report being more forgetful than moms-to-be carrying boys, though the science is dicey.",
        "text_sha256": "b3802457728dcf99cc220af8912fcebd3c7a63cb2c683f0450f53966b8183de2",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Shortness of breath",
        "text_sha256": "6a4244d09915820f384c892b52fe03e6c761da63725e3e2f4692c41c726e8eff",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "That burgeoning belly is pushing anything out of its way — including your lungs, which can’t fully expand. It’s more uncomfortable for you than it is for your baby, who is getting the oxygen he needs from the placenta. What helps? Standing as straight as you can so that your lungs have a little more room.",
        "text_sha256": "5128af4fc43088d3bcf8bba6119f84600dd657d124e43a45da701231b8b95ab6",
        "word_count": 56
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000044",
        "block_index": 44,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Braxton Hicks contractions",
        "text_sha256": "5285eeccdcc1c0a12b62bcdb1665797e938e1a2ac1ba0d3d5f0d33a4d9a51bf5",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000045",
        "block_index": 45,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "These practice contractions are most often felt by moms who’ve already gone through a pregnancy. How do you know they’re not the real thing? Even at their most intense, changing your position — from sitting to lying down, from lying down to walking around — will usually make them disappear.",
        "text_sha256": "1ee0287962de016883a09d4961fe57b730871dd9ad7f830bac4d380a980c6e6c",
        "word_count": 50
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000046",
        "block_index": 46,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Clumsiness",
        "text_sha256": "b6879f1ae4a2888c8d96d1c91048e8e6d19d7316ab7ebedfaa0480eb073ffd66",
        "word_count": 1
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000047",
        "block_index": 47,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A bigger belly means a shift in your center of gravity and that can spell clumsiness. What to do about it? Slow down and take it easy — rushing will only make you clumsier.",
        "text_sha256": "ec1aba666601cf9b1b3b906cca961ed5d34aee14ae07804c2f44979eab8d4ade",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000048",
        "block_index": 48,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Nail changes",
        "text_sha256": "9b9f8bbe57c9b4ed9d0ff41783267fc8d77fc893fe137526775ca06f99eba74a",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000049",
        "block_index": 49,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Pregnancy hormones can make nails grow faster but can also cause them to become brittle. If your nails are weak and break easily, try getting lots of biotin in your diet in the form of bananas, avocados, nuts, and whole grains.",
        "text_sha256": "5296b7d239e575396a413194569d89ed8833bb56f2f4c6353ba8ecf56e5284fe",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000050",
        "block_index": 50,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Varicose veins",
        "text_sha256": "ffa2cbf469d247a7e4946eba7b2ee403ddd5c3a14f91357bfafbde5235a52d1f",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000051",
        "block_index": 51,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Sure, they’re not pretty — and sometimes they can be painful — but if you’re worried that your varicose veins can be harmful too, you can relax. The good news? If you didn’t have them before you got pregnant, they’ll disappear soon after you give birth.",
        "text_sha256": "05ff3b440b3f1a97e75962bdb886e3334d07a1e0afa9247574171f830d37ad19",
        "word_count": 46
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000052",
        "block_index": 52,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Strong fetal movement",
        "text_sha256": "205e1125e8baf1de069b9a8b920550043d997e5ccc039eba7849578ff28ecf1c",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000053",
        "block_index": 53,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can test for fetal movement twice a day — in the morning and evening. Check the clock and start counting every wiggle, roll, kick, and flutter until you reach 10. If by the end of the hour you haven’t felt at least that many movements, have a snack or some juice, lie down, and resume counting. Your baby just may have needed an energy boost too!",
        "text_sha256": "65be82af381f1f505fec31d5c56bd2cbe2147c7be99d232a5d85dd53b7b6d441",
        "word_count": 67
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000054",
        "block_index": 54,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Tips for You This Week",
        "text_sha256": "32770aa6fb9708009741b1a7b10691529ec1e23f79116914a353fdf3f2baf4e7",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000055",
        "block_index": 55,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Look into breastfeeding",
        "text_sha256": "97574f24046c22ac4eb83fd63c961cb8da2fa1c47f6e170da319337820ff73e7",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000056",
        "block_index": 56,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "About 83% of women try breastfeeding after giving birth , according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Will you be one of them?",
        "text_sha256": "4ce8edad4987cc57b6e4dc3723c43650d6830342bd21d8c6623706b7611248d0",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000057",
        "block_index": 57,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": ", according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Will you be one of them?",
        "text_sha256": "d85d80759be5ac0298479d19c3e3e74c683b16323d104953f903692ca77041b5",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000058",
        "block_index": 58,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If so, now’s a great time to talk to your practitioner about it, watch some instructional videos, read up on it, or even take a breastfeeding class if you're able to.",
        "text_sha256": "24477f88e90e418b83d5e0a2b368bcd7e3c17ada484503e0c2da68010243186e",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000059",
        "block_index": 59,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Thinking about room-sharing?",
        "text_sha256": "ca59e0701fbb4f842f15cc25ddd40f4762b8c7c3cb1f55390edce4574633b00d",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000060",
        "block_index": 60,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Find out your hospital’s policy on rooming-in — sharing a room with your baby 24/7 after you give birth.",
        "text_sha256": "7d952f884a29b1ca2fb8da924a729a522db71d27545434a6fed127b674f8bf05",
        "word_count": 19
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000061",
        "block_index": 61,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "on rooming-in — sharing a room with your baby 24/7 after you give birth.",
        "text_sha256": "b784e7bdb511b7413196fbd8c121a280149853546fc658756d59e34d80436135",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000062",
        "block_index": 62,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Though it's completely your choice, it can be a good experience for you and your baby if you want to give it a try. But it’s okay if you decide not to room-share too!",
        "text_sha256": "371542cf643ac2b0b472431e4b71da004fc6b02dca069bee27c862736c023083",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000063",
        "block_index": 63,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Narrow down your list of names",
        "text_sha256": "d0e6afe6395249462b4aa15f74f480b4d0c9f81c9215c5e8fb14c756009f9114",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000064",
        "block_index": 64,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Choosing a name for your baby is one of the biggest early decisions you’ll make as a parent. Will you select a popular name or a more unique one? Will you go with a traditional boy or girl’s name or a gender-neutral one ?",
        "text_sha256": "4b3c93b0816119a9a2b70447a72adc0fb062a4723ead8d0bbb8ae80f7fd278a5",
        "word_count": 44
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000065",
        "block_index": 65,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Choosing a name for your baby is one of the biggest early decisions you’ll make as a parent. Will you select a popular name or a more unique one? Will you go with a traditional",
        "text_sha256": "15774a7f9a2bde446cd4bf3c23686bad331568a3e52faf9964d3e0a968771c8f",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000066",
        "block_index": 66,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Think about how the name will sound with your child’s last name (or names), and what the initials will spell. And consider the shortened form of your top selections — your child may pick up a nickname whether you like it or not!",
        "text_sha256": "74531c151a8cf43c27a853db81437df5c704eba32b76731931cdc398c2e8805b",
        "word_count": 43
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000067",
        "block_index": 67,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Switch to light weights",
        "text_sha256": "fabb0a6da6df6959aaf5e05af6d0724d57150145560a337e896ad6f228f45ea6",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000068",
        "block_index": 68,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Yes, weight training increases muscle tone — and even prevents bone loss — but during pregnancy it's important to lighten up.",
        "text_sha256": "85ec3cf7ecfedb3da25566cc139d40e720506b9ac81721f1652badf0e457e17a",
        "word_count": 21
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000069",
        "block_index": 69,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "increases muscle tone — and even prevents bone loss — but during pregnancy it's important to lighten up.",
        "text_sha256": "43a78e3f5d7f5ea3ada1d188d92f30c0975b490d9a47e707a117dcb49ccba604",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000070",
        "block_index": 70,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Lifting heavy weights increases pressure in your body, which causes you to hold your breath and could compromise blood flow to the uterus. Plus, loosened ligaments could lead to injury.",
        "text_sha256": "e9355020d72a2d6c06bd8fe22a57843b8630667bb5c394e1df0796cd4d91322c",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000071",
        "block_index": 71,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your best bet: Switch to light weights and do more reps — or just wait on weights until after delivery.",
        "text_sha256": "7e344832fe5c5d87b7b75ff7ab33953bea6f0f272f08bd3b7afa61ddde6f561f",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000072",
        "block_index": 72,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You've got so many hopes and dreams about your baby, and each one is worth remembering and sharing. Record them before you forget by writing a letter — or a series of letters — to your baby.",
        "text_sha256": "fb6554cf31d4001e95073bf523efe9b8677e1ecb6a041b06322d515eda339492",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000073",
        "block_index": 73,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You've got so many hopes and dreams about your baby, and each one is worth remembering and sharing.",
        "text_sha256": "5079dc20dfe538da090a1c8ec8fa075ad5be116e64a0d3e184974c473426602d",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000074",
        "block_index": 74,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "before you forget by writing a letter — or a series of letters — to your baby.",
        "text_sha256": "007e3bc9c2ec89e62cf5899a61fd0a09d31f07819da14c7560d49b65d85b5319",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000075",
        "block_index": 75,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Feel a little funny writing to a fetus? Just speak from the heart. Talk about what this pregnancy means to you and how it's changing your body and your world. Recount the foods you craved, the way you finally chose your baby’s name, and what colors you picked for the nursery.",
        "text_sha256": "91dbfa1cb75e3e68d2b265bce3e9c00d526b60999403be68745fb92b64784261",
        "word_count": 51
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000076",
        "block_index": 76,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Share your reaction when you first found out you were pregnant, felt those first kicks, and found out your baby's sex (if you have). Imagine what you'll do together in the future. Do you see yourself pushing a swing in the park or tossing a football on the front lawn? Or imagine what your baby's future might be like.",
        "text_sha256": "e6a8f2fd908dd06c7d287b3f5b709fd90ccd28de5cfa5f95b0e91bdd9bd6ce2d",
        "word_count": 59
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000077",
        "block_index": 77,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your letter to your baby is certain to become one of your most treasured possessions — and eventually, your child's.",
        "text_sha256": "97edefe0739616e579bd47fcc53790494df7db763e7913dbf824cb80d0852d14",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000078",
        "block_index": 78,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Prevent an upset stomach",
        "text_sha256": "42199b59e54429f98462ce10689252b2a1b336a555a50b1e82aac0c2b0afafde",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000079",
        "block_index": 79,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your body has trouble digesting lactose (a type of sugar), you may find that drinking milk can lead to cramps, gas, bloating, and diarrhea .",
        "text_sha256": "6b71d72e081ed40b644ff9f1dafef94addd4a33e3d4c98ea545fd20a4e4a3dfd",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000080",
        "block_index": 80,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Sure, you could buy lactose-free milk or pop a lactase capsule before downing a glass of the stuff, but there are other ways to crank up your calcium consumption and avoid an upset stomach.",
        "text_sha256": "1a9fbc55f2bb6bb14325740c2a9fa99dbea281617e1bb9e2cfbbcdf8dc3caea2",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000081",
        "block_index": 81,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "One solution : Get active with active yogurt cultures. They harbor good bacteria that break down lactose. Or, eat naturally aged hard cheeses like cheddar, Parmesan, and Swiss, which lose more than half their lactose during processing. Just make sure they’re pasteurized — and most cheeses in the U.S. are — since unpasteurized ones can be contaminated with listeria , a harmful bacterium.",
        "text_sha256": "bf13caeffd17e15dd50c79fc5cf5d57f1bc5aa064976a3a7829800c89ca50015",
        "word_count": 63
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000082",
        "block_index": 82,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": ": Get active with active yogurt cultures. They harbor good bacteria that break down lactose. Or, eat naturally aged hard",
        "text_sha256": "b9b8af4c5215dfcb709434a9096a13dac8935df0d7dcabc32b38fe433378d06a",
        "word_count": 20
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000083",
        "block_index": 83,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "like cheddar, Parmesan, and Swiss, which lose more than half their lactose during processing. Just make sure they’re pasteurized — and most cheeses in the U.S. are — since unpasteurized ones can be contaminated with",
        "text_sha256": "79874487eaff25324ab92c2e36dffb689de72fb4d62ffdebcef02cf01a9b851c",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000084",
        "block_index": 84,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can also cut back on the amount of milk you drink. It’s possible that your stomach can handle smaller servings, like a half a cup at one sitting or a thin slice of mozzarella.",
        "text_sha256": "3fa3cc99c19f82f27312cdfc06acdba256cb7d1cde575c6d4ddc2c0e124e793c",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000085",
        "block_index": 85,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Lastly, since lactose is easier to digest when mixed with other foods, particularly high-fiber ones, take your milk with whole grain cereal, or melt that cheese on a slice of whole wheat bread.",
        "text_sha256": "259fdfb2e9b30ba674c29d654a28f529fefc805b48947bce11db29773f87c21d",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000086",
        "block_index": 86,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Sleep on your side",
        "text_sha256": "3e9b7e7001ee811f8b63df5591999af183b33c21120750850a65ff603cd80d3b",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000087",
        "block_index": 87,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It’s been a little while since you’ve been able to sleep on your back — or your belly, for that matter — but now that you're officially a side sleeper, consider rolling onto your left side at night (though either side works).",
        "text_sha256": "39aaac421b64f76c995d08099aaa0fa247f93b971c2c422aa3c39fb3bf439c69",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000088",
        "block_index": 88,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Why? Some experts say that the left side is the ideal sleeping position for both you and your baby, since it allows for maximum blood flow and nutrients to the placenta. It may also help ease the swelling in your feet, ankles, and hands.",
        "text_sha256": "ba28a052424a206070434a4ad2062a7831c0c6dc4d7744c39a3d342aa96572a8",
        "word_count": 44
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000089",
        "block_index": 89,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "for both you and your baby, since it allows for maximum blood flow and nutrients to the placenta. It may also help ease the swelling in your feet, ankles, and hands.",
        "text_sha256": "1408a8016c057dcc6e1b5798fdb3c1cd6ffc290636c9c58a2d3f8de299201e2e",
        "word_count": 31
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000090",
        "block_index": 90,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you do roll onto your back during the night or just can’t get comfortable on your side, tilt your hips to one side and place a pillow under one side of your bottom for support.",
        "text_sha256": "0e43d3097d490a244c2ebc75f3aea237fd2689afeb3349ef2f4ca2a06ca6d092",
        "word_count": 36
      }
    ],
